---
name: type-ascription-unit
description: "case _: Unit matches the unit value, and it must mean the same on every lane"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# `case _: Unit`

`BUGS.md` `v2-native-case-unit-pattern-matches-where-int-does-not`. `Unit` is an ordinary
type and a type-ascription pattern on it holds for the unit value. The native lane already
agreed — its type-test table maps the unit value to the `Unit` tag — while INT and JS both
fell through to the wildcard, because their tables listed every scalar type *except* this
one and ended in a catch-all `false`.

That the two disagreed matters more than the pattern itself: **INT is this suite's
reference lane**, so any case written around `case _: Unit` was being graded against the
lane that got it wrong.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Unit => "unit"
  case _: Int => "int"
  case _ => "other"

println(kind(()))
println(kind(5))
println(kind("s"))
```

A function whose body ends in `println` returns unit, so the pattern has to hold for a
*computed* unit as well as the `()` literal — that is the shape the defect was found in
(a `match` on the result of a side-effecting call, which then printed `()` on INT and
nothing on native).

```scalascript
def side(): Unit = println("effect")

def f(x: Any): Unit =
  x match
    case _: Unit => ()
    case _ => println(x)

f(side())
println("after")
```

The wildcard must still win for everything that is not unit, or "fixed" would just mean
the arm swallowed every scrutinee.

```scalascript
val xs = List((), 1, "a", true)
println(xs.map(x => kind(x)).mkString(","))
```
